  "question": "Background / Context\n\nGravity is a SaaS boilerplate framework developed to facilitate the creation of full-stack web applications using a Node.js backend with a React frontend. It aims to streamline the development of SaaS applications by providing pre-configured solutions for common, complex tasks such as payments and authentication.\n\nObjectives\n\nThe primary objective is to enable developers to quickly build and deploy SaaS applications. By handling routine back-end configurations, Gravity allows developers to focus on implementing unique features that can drive business value and user engagement.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Primary users who will utilize the boilerplate to develop custom SaaS applications.\n- End Users of SaaS Applications: Indirect stakeholders, benefiting from the applications developed using the boilerplate.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n- Server-Side Configuration:\n  - Utilize Express for API handling.\n  - Implement robust error handling and environment configuration.\n  - Leverage Knex as a query builder for SQL operations.\n  - Support database migrations for consistent schema evolution.\n  - Enable concurrent task handling.\n\n- Client-Side Configuration:\n  - Employ React for building the user interface.\n  - Use React Router for navigation.\n  - Provide a global view component and a 404 error view.\n  - Include reusable UI components like buttons, cards, links, and logos.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Performance: The system should be optimized to handle multiple requests simultaneously.\n- Scalability: Designed to support scalable growth as user demand increases.\n- Maintainability: The code should be organized and documented to facilitate easy updates and modifications.\n- Security: Payment and authentication processes should adhere to best security practices.\n- Usability: The framework should be intuitive for developers to integrate and extend according to specific business needs.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0048\nCREATE TABLE `framework_components` (\n  `component_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `component_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `component_type` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_reusable` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`component_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `framework_features` (\n  `feature_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `feature_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `feature_category` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_enabled` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`feature_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `framework_stakeholders` (\n  `stakeholder_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `stakeholder_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `stakeholder_type` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`stakeholder_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `framework_requirements` (\n  `requirement_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `requirement_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `requirement_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `priority` varchar(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'medium',\n  `is_mandatory` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`requirement_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `feature_components` (\n  `feature_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `component_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`feature_id`,`component_id`),\n  KEY `feature_components_ibfk_2` (`component_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `feature_components_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`feature_id`) REFERENCES `framework_features` (`feature_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `feature_components_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`component_id`) REFERENCES `framework_components` (`component_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `feature_requirements` (\n  `feature_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `requirement_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`feature_id`,`requirement_id`),\n  KEY `feature_requirements_ibfk_2` (`requirement_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `feature_requirements_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`feature_id`) REFERENCES `framework_features` (`feature_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `feature_requirements_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`requirement_id`) REFERENCES `framework_requirements` (`requirement_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `stakeholder_requirements` (\n  `stakeholder_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `requirement_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`stakeholder_id`,`requirement_id`),\n  KEY `stakeholder_requirements_ibfk_2` (`requirement_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `stakeholder_requirements_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`stakeholder_id`) REFERENCES `framework_stakeholders` (`stakeholder_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `stakeholder_requirements_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`requirement_id`) REFERENCES `framework_requirements` (`requirement_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
